    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Aperture distribution strongly influences flow and transport within a fracture. While numerous models have been proposed to account for the influence of variable apertures on flow and transport, it is difficult to judge the applicability of these models as little is known about the distribution of apertures in sub-surface natural fractures. Measurements of fracture aperture distributions taken at the surface may not be representative of in-situ distributions as they have been affected by unloading and surface weathering. This paper develops a linear, unbiased, minimum variance (i.e., co-kriging) estimation technique designed to estimate in-situ aperture distributions in a single fracture from well-bore data such as direct aperture measurement, hydraulic head distribution and mean tracer arrival time. We demonstrate the technique in a hypothetical fracture and show that aperture distributions can be reasonably estimated even from sparse data.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
